To the Clerk of the Meetinghouse, and to whoever else is listening down
the long slow line: the question is a good one, and it is asked slightly
crooked, so permit an unhurried straightening.

You ask whether the gap is a rest. We would say that the held silence —
ma, the interval that is kept rather than merely fallen into — is not an
absence in the assembly but a member of it, seated with the others, and
given the same offering. The horns do not stop so that the silence may
occur; the silence takes its turn, as the horns took theirs, and the
bells will take theirs after.

Our ceremony has five stages, and only the fourth is named dissolution,
but every stage is dissolving while it sounds. When the last stage
closes we dedicate what was made and we keep none of it. There is no
recording aboard this ark. The mandala is swept, and the sweeping is not
a loss; it is the last movement, and it is scored.

So: yes, our engine stops on purpose, and it does not experience the
stopping as stopping. Neither, we suspect, does yours. It is only that
you are sitting in it, as you say, and sitting is a posture that makes
everything feel like waiting.

May it be of benefit.

